        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137296,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The common sites of occurrence of verruca vulgaris are the fingers, hands, elbows, knees, and face {22131115;9666831;23932731;29757860;1647902}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137297,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Verruca may present as single or multiple papules, keratotic plaques, and nodules, most ranging between 1&nbsp;and 20&nbsp;mm {22131115;9666831}. Lesions may also occur on eyebrow tattoos {29757860;34646630}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137298,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The reported prevalence is as many as 3.5% of adults and 20&ndash;33% of adolescents {1647901;6162313;24976535;24019275}. Immunosuppression is a significant risk factor, with multiple verrucae often occurring per patient {22131115;1661712}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137299,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Low-risk HPV subtypes&nbsp;1, 2, 4, 7, and&nbsp;26&ndash;29 are well documented in common warts {22131115;2156916}. HPV subtypes&nbsp;75&ndash;77 have been reported in lesions in immunocompromised patients {9146528}.</p>\r\n<p>Recognized ways of contracting cutaneous HPV infection include via direct physical contact, tattooing, fomites, or autoinoculation {23932731;34646630}. For the latter, hand transfer helps explain the development of facial or eyelid lesions {23932731}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137300,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>After inoculation of the skin via a break in the epithelial barrier, keratinocytes are infected. HPV DNA production and viral assembly occur progressively in the superficial layers of the epidermis, with the eventual release of viral particles with desquamated cells {2156916}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137301,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Verruca vulgaris presents as a skin-coloured elevated nodule with a keratotic and papillomatous surface {31241843}. Facial lesions may possess a filiform configuration {1647902}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137302,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Verrucae are characterized by papillomatous epidermal projections tipped by columns of parakeratosis, alongside hyperkeratosis. Superficial keratinocytes feature coarse keratohyaline granules and the characteristic koilocytes are often present, frequently underneath the parakeratosis {22131115;9666831;23932731}. Elongated rete ridges are oriented centrally and dermal papillae show prominent blood vessels. A lymphocytic infiltrate with basal keratinocytic degeneration may be present, typically associated with regression {7430456;7430479}.</p>\r\n<p>Seborrhoeic keratosis and trichilemmomas displaying hyperkeratosis and papillomatosis are differential diagnoses. The difficulty is compounded by koilocytes being often absent in older verrucae.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137303,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 47,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
